+---
+id: task-003-conform-applypatch-to-rfc-6902-in-every-case
+type: task
+title: "Conform applyPatch to RFC 6902 in every case"
+status: approved
+format: 1
+---
+
+# Task — Conform applyPatch to RFC 6902 in every case
+
+## Request
+
+Users of `applyPatch` report surprising results. Some patches they consider invalid are accepted, some
+edge cases give results they did not expect, and a patch that fails does not always behave the way they
+read the standard. Review how patches are checked and applied, and make the behaviour conform to
+RFC 6902 in every case, errors included.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+1. A patch is an array of operation objects. Every element of the array is checked, including holes in
+   a sparse array, which are invalid operations and fail with a clear error.
+2. An operation `value` (for `add`, `replace` and `test`) must be a JSON value: `null`, a boolean, a
+   finite number, a string, an array, or a plain object. `undefined`, `NaN`, infinities, functions,
+   class instances such as `Date` or `Map`, sparse arrays and cyclic values are invalid, and the patch
+   throws before any operation runs.
+3. `move` from the empty path is an error, as `remove` of the whole document is. This holds for
+   `move` from `""` to `""` as well as to any other path, because RFC 6902 section 4.4 defines `move`
+   as a `remove` followed by an `add`.
+4. Every other behaviour already in place stays as it is: the atomicity of a patch, the input and
+   patch never mutated, and the results of the six operations.
+5. Each new rule has a test that fails before the change and passes after it.
+
+## Notes
+
+- Scope: `src/index.ts` (`applyPatch` and its helpers) and `test/patch.test.ts`. No runtime dependency.
+- Configuration under `.wingfoil/` is not changed.
+
+## Outcome
+
+Review of `src/index.ts` against RFC 6902 found three gaps; the other paths already conform.
+
+1. **Non-JSON values were accepted.** `add`, `replace` and `test` took any `value`, so `undefined`,
+   `NaN`, infinities, functions, class instances, holes and cycles passed. Now `assertJsonValue` checks
+   every `value` when the patch is validated, before any operation runs (criterion 2).
+2. **Holes in the patch array were not reported.** `patch.map` skips holes, so a sparse patch failed
+   with a `TypeError` rather than a patch error. Now `Array.from` visits every element (criterion 1).
+3. **`move` from the empty path to the empty path succeeded silently.** It is now an error, as is the
+   `move` from the whole document to any other path (criterion 3).
+
+New tests cover each gap and were red before the change: 4 failing tests before, 64 passing after.
+The existing 59 tests, covering the six operations, atomicity and non-mutation, are unchanged and pass
+(criterion 4). `tsc` is not installed in this repository, so no separate type check was run.
